> I was able to find it myself! In case anyone else is curious, it's at: https://cran.r-project.org/web/packages/lavaan/lavaan.pdf

The documentation is also built into R, and easily accessible by typing the function name into the console.

?lavaan

> The variables I ordered actually aren't categorical variables, however whenever I try to run an SEM R gives the error: " lavaan ERROR: unordered factor(s) with more than 2 levels detected as exogenous covariate(s)"

Unordered factors are nominal variables, which are not supported by lavaan as endogenous variables (i.e., outcomes).  But these are exogenous (i.e., they are only predictors), so you can make dummy codes to represent them.

http://lavaan.ugent.be/tutorial/cat.html

> Do you know why it might be thinking they are categorical variables?

That must be how they are stored.  Check any variable's class:

class(mydata$X1FAMINCOME)

Or the whole data.frame at once:

sapply(mydata, class)

> They are mostly scored on a scale from 1-4 but there is also a lot of missing data (but it's already declared as missing in SPSS).

If you imported an SPSS data file using foreign::read.spss(), try setting the arguments

use.value.labels = FALSE, to.data.frame = TRUE

That will then import the numeric values, rather than importing the labels (which therefore must be considered categorical).

