Age might be a problem, because its scale seems huge relative to other variables.  Is it measured in days or something?  You could divide it by a constant to reduce its variance (e.g., divide by 10 to express its effect for every 10-unit increase).  That should make sure its variance doesn't dominate the parameter space while the optimizer tries to find a solution.  Also, why are you regressing age on anything, much less on gender, and setting that effect to 0?  Did you mean to simply force the covariance between gender and age to be 0?  Use a double-tilde for covariances.  But age and gender should be exogenous, so just don't fix it to zero (there is no reason for that restriction, and it will only decrease model fit to the degree that the sample covariance is not zero).

Also, I doubt that "Diet" is actually a common factor.  It seems like a composite.  Do some searching about formative vs. reflective measurement.  You can also ask on SEMNET, which is a general forum with more participation than this little software-help forum:

www2.gsu.edu/~mkteer/semnet.html <http://www2.gsu.edu/~mkteer/semnet.html>

If the correlations among the Diet indicators are not following the pattern that would at least allow a common factor to be "faked" in your model, then I am guessing that is causing the convergence problems, because your model would probably put too much constraint on the relationships of each indicator with other outcomes that you use "Diet" to predict.

Terrence D. Jorgensen
Assistant Professor, Methods and Statistics
