> only the variance estimates on the actual items.  The rest of of the 3 columns were left blank for these items.  Why is that??

They are fixed for identification, not estimated.

> (2) ... Do you know why?

See above, and find more details about the DWLS approach here:

https://www.statmodel.com/download/webnotes/CatMGLong.pdf

> Is that the 42 thresholds approach #1 estimates that approach #2 doesn't

Yes.

> is it the "scales y*", which were all estimated to be 1s?

Again, they were fixed for identification, for the same reason you have to fix a common-factor variance (or loading per factor) to 1 for identification.

