Hi,

I have some questions about differences between interaction and moderation mediation in lavaan syntax.

1) in the regressions below, am I only testing interaction?

vez.fr <http://vez.fr>: is the product of indicators I made with indProd matching "fr" with "vez"

out  ~ e*fr + d*mo + a1*at + k*vez + z*vez.fr #interaction only?
fr   ~ a*at
mo   ~ b*at + c*fr

2) if "vez.fr <http://vez.fr>" is significant, but "vez" is not, is there an interaction anyway?

If it is an interaction, and if I want to check for moderated mediation instead  (so if the mediating effect of "fr" on the relationship between "at" and "out": at-->fr-->out is moderated by vez [the moderator is between fr and out]), I should look at the indirect effects (defining additional parameters), is it correct? . In my case:

ind_1:= a*z #moderated mediation?

3) so is ind_1 measuring the moderated mediation effect?

4) if the ind_1 path is significant, must the direct effects of "vez" and of "vez.fr <http://vez.fr>" on "out" be significant too to affirm that there is a moderated mediation?

5) if I want to check the direction of these relationship (interaction and moderated mediation), should I just look at the sign of the non-standardized coefficients (column "Estimate") of the moderator vez.fr <http://vez.fr>?

Thank you very much,

Fran

From u3fe90052fbf2f899 at groups.google.com  Sat Aug 31 08:22:55 2019
From: u3fe90052fbf2f899 at groups.google.com (Terrence Jorgensen)
Date: Sat, 31 Aug 2019 08:22:55 +0000
Subject: Re: Interaction vs moderated mediation: check on lavaan syntax
Message-ID: <q6NHonlqmHw.Y_duV7IDAQAJ@lavaan.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <q6NHonlqmHw.KGQlitjOAAAJ@lavaan.groups.google.com>
References: <q6NHonlqmHw.KGQlitjOAAAJ@lavaan.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/lavaan/c/q6NHonlqmHw/m/Y_duV7IDAQAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

> 1) in the regressions below, am I only testing interaction?

Looks like mediation and moderation.  Read this article about models with both types of effects, and draw a diagram of your model to make sure you understand what you are doing:

http://quantpsy.org/pubs/preacher_rucker_hayes_2007.pdf

> vez.fr <http://vez.fr>: is the product of indicators I made with indProd matching "fr" with "vez"

To be clear, indProd() is only meant for creating multiple product indicators for modeling latent interactions.  For interactions among observed variables, you should just create the product term yourself (without any centering, so that the effect can be interpreted correctly; see the primer I link to below).

> 2) if "vez.fr <http://vez.fr>" is significant, but "vez" is not, is there an interaction anyway?

z is the interaction effect, indicating that the effect of vez changes as fr changes.  k is just a simple slope.  Read this primer about interpreting moderation:

http://quantpsy.org/interact/interactions.htm

> If it is an interaction, and if I want to check for moderated mediation instead  (so if the mediating effect of "fr" on the relationship between "at" and "out": at-->fr-->out is moderated by vez [the moderator is between fr and out]), I should look at the indirect effects (defining additional parameters), is it correct? . In my case:
>
> ind_1:= a*z #moderated mediation?
>

You have a more complicated situation because your moderator is a mediator.  The article I linked to above gives 5 examples of moderated mediation models, and you can probably find one of the path diagrams is consistent with your own (when you draw yours).

Terrence D. Jorgensen
Assistant Professor, Methods and Statistics
Research Institute for Child Development and Education, the University of Amsterdam
http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen

